<h2 id="qwenlab-trains-smaller-transformers-inside-the-model">qwenLab trains smaller transformers inside the model</h2>

<p>qwenLab appends 2,382 constructed layers to Qwen3.5-9B. The lab holds six experiments, each with a small transformer learning addition modulo 97. Its layers maintain learner weights and optimizer state, run forward and backward passes, apply AdamW updates, and evaluate the learners. The base Qwen model chooses commands and settings under a supplied protocol. The system therefore has two levels of learning behavior: the small transformers update their parameters while Qwen selects experiments with its own weights fixed. <a href="https://github.com/DeepakSingh98/qwenlab">qwenLab repository</a></p>

<p>In the reported session, the best selected fork achieved 49.09% test accuracy versus 15.68% for the control. That result comes from one run with a fixed protocol and a narrow search over learning rate and weight decay. The authors report about 0.64 seconds per internal training update versus 5.5 milliseconds for the same update in PyTorch on the same H100. The demonstration shows how much of a training workflow can run inside a model, including branching an experiment and comparing its results. <a href="https://github.com/DeepakSingh98/qwenlab#results">qwenLab results and limits</a></p>

