<h2 id="spotlight-gives-a-fixed-executor-an-extensible-memory">Spotlight gives a fixed executor an extensible memory</h2>

<p>Percepta’s second article asks what an executor can gain by keeping code and data in writable memory. Its Spotlight architecture addresses a small number of memory cells per token, so the amount of memory accessed at each step can stay fixed as the stored state grows. The authors use a hand-built Python environment to demonstrate package loading and replacement. The same execution module can run a different procedure after its memory changes. <a href="https://www.percepta.ai/blog/can-llms-grow-their-own-capabilities">Percepta, Can LLMs Grow Their Own Capabilities?</a></p>

<p>One demonstration stores the weights of a digit classifier in memory. The execution module reads the weights in chunks and evaluates the classifier. This is a concrete example of capability growth through loading a procedure or model into memory. The article’s longer-term proposal is that a system could also discover, validate, and retain its own methods over time. The package and classifier examples demonstrate the mechanism for using added capabilities. <a href="https://www.percepta.ai/blog/can-llms-grow-their-own-capabilities">Percepta, Can LLMs Grow Their Own Capabilities?</a></p>

