<h2 id="sketchpad-makes-local-arithmetic-exhaustively-checkable">Sketchpad makes local arithmetic exhaustively checkable</h2>

<p>In <em>Sketchpad: certified exact arithmetic with a small vision-language model</em>, Mostafa “Neo” Mohsenvand adapts Qwen3.5-0.8B to work on a rendered sheet. Each step reads three cells and produces the arithmetic output and next operation. Conventional code writes those outputs and moves the view; the model computes the result digits. The low-rank adapter learns the local operations, while the runtime supplies the procedures and working memory. <a href="https://github.com/NeoVand/sketchpad">Sketchpad repository</a></p>

<p>The important restriction is that no conversation history or evolving hidden state carries information between steps. Everything needed for the next step is supplied through its bounded input. A longer multiplication requires more steps, but it does not introduce an unlimited variety of local cases.</p>

<p>That makes exhaustive checking possible. The paper enumerates <strong>2,446 operation-specific views</strong> and proves in Lean that correct local outputs imply correct runs of any length, provided the runtime presents only covered views and its reference procedures are correct. All views pass on the reported Apple M4 browser deployment. The runtime’s view coverage and the correctness of its classical arithmetic procedures are <strong>not themselves formalized in Lean</strong>. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2611.04434#S3">Paper, Sections 2–3</a></p>

