<p>The project reports 220 correct answers on 220 pre-registered long-addition and multiplication problems. That benchmark illustrates the system; the finite-view argument supplies the guarantee under its assumptions. The dialogue version still has to choose and transcribe a computation, and its reported performance on fresh GSM8K problems falls 5.8 points below the base model. Certified execution does not certify that the right problem was posed. <a href="https://github.com/NeoVand/sketchpad#results">Results and limitations</a></p>

<p>The <a href="https://neovand.github.io/sketchpad/demo">original interactive demo</a> shows the sheet, local views, and model outputs. It starts with recorded runs; loading the model enables execution on the reader’s GPU. Its default device check covers the weakest tenth of views; a full check is available separately. <a href="https://neovand.github.io/sketchpad/demo">Demo and device-check behavior</a></p>

<h2 id="where-a-reusable-capability-lives">Where a reusable capability lives</h2>

<p>These projects connect to a question from my earlier post on computational words. Suppose a model identifies a repeated procedure and gives it a name. The name makes it easier to refer to the procedure. A separate design decision determines what happens when the name is used. <a href="/machine%20learning/computer%20science/creating-new-words/">Creating New Words</a></p>

<p>A compiler can build the procedure’s execution rules into layers. An interpreter can run code supplied as tokens. A memory architecture can keep that code available across invocations and replace it when a better version arrives. From this perspective, qwenLab illustrates how a model can choose among actions offered by a built-in subsystem, while Spotlight illustrates how the set of available procedures can change after the executor is built. Sketchpad adds a different question: can every local decision in a learned procedure be checked, so that confidence in execution no longer depends only on sampled problems?</p>

<p>The distinction matters when evaluating a claim that a model gained a capability. Running a supplied program tests execution. Keeping that program available tests storage and retrieval. Discovering a program worth retaining, verifying it, and applying it to a new problem would test something further. Each step can be evaluated on its own, which gives a more precise account of what improved and where that improvement resides.</p>

<h2 id="appendix-what-sketchpads-certificate-covers">Appendix: what Sketchpad’s certificate covers</h2>

<p>The certificate is tied to the model files, glyphs, precision, and execution path. A different deployment needs a full check or a justified bound on numerical changes. The paper tests sampled batch compositions, rather than exhaustively bounding every possible batch. Its guarantee concerns the restricted sheet machine; it does not show that a finite benchmark certifies an unrestricted chat model. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2611.04434#S8">Certification methods and discussion</a></p>
